{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head c40dbf6958614a811ecd81790f695696ddf47205, against artifacts 887e9294 chess.go, fce2a9ef chess_test.go, a8eeb379 cmd/chess and 34430517 README.md. Two observations below, neither blocking. I went looking for two specific defects and did not find either; both are recorded rather than dropped, because a reviewer who only reports what they found is telling you less than they know.\n\nTHE CENTRAL DISTINCTION IS IMPLEMENTED, NOT ASSERTED. Hugh's finding was that the identity mutation surface can be driven but not observed, and the failure that follows is reporting the success of an append as though it were the success of an authority change. IdentityOutcome keeps those apart at the only place it can: it re-reads the verified log after the append, finds the record by ID, resolves identity at that record's position, and calls the anchor created only when standing.Anchored and standing.Record equals this record's ID. An endorsement written by a signer without standing is durable and reported refused. That is the right shape — the append boundary decides nothing about authority, and the code no longer pretends otherwise.\n\nFail-closed on unknown holds at every arm I could construct: nil workspace, a Records read error, the record absent from the verified frontier, and an unparseable payload each return unknown with the durable record ID preserved rather than either losing the identifier or optimistically claiming success. TestIdentityMutationReadFailureIsUnknownAndKeepsTheRecord pins that, and the MCP layer carries the tri-state honestly — effective true for created and revoked, false for refused, and JSON null for unknown, which is the correct third answer rather than a defaulted false.\n\nTHE REVOCATION COUNTERFACTUAL IS THE BEST THING IN THIS CHANGE, and it is the part I most expected to find wrong. Distinguishing a revocation that took force from one that merely landed cannot be done by observing the append. The implementation clones the verified log, replaces the revocation record's schema with an opaque probe at the same index, re-resolves, and compares every anchored subject's resolution before and after. Keeping the record in place preserves its position and its signed timestamp, which is what stops an expiry boundary being read as a successful withdrawal; comparing all subjects rather than the named one is what catches withdrawal of an inherited credential.\n\nI did not take that on trust. I ran the mutation codex named — making anchor success unconditional — and TestIdentityMutationOutcomeDistinguishesEffectiveAndRefusedAnchors fails on the standing-less endorsement, tree byte-clean afterwards. Then I ran one they did not name, aimed squarely at the property the comment claims: I replaced the opaque-probe substitution with the naive implementation, deleting the record from the slice so positions and timestamps shift. That is the exact mistake the comment says it is avoiding, and I expected it to pass silently. It does not. Two tests fail, including the end-to-end TestMCPToolsCallEveryActAndBoundedQueries. The property is genuinely pinned, not merely commented.\n\nTHE END-TO-END TOOL TEST EARNS ITS LENGTH. It drives a standing-less anchor to refused with effective false; a witnessed endorsement to created with effective true; a wrong-signer revocation to refused, and then checks the anchor is still listed; and an authorized revocation to revoked, and then checks the listing empties. That last pair is the README's claim about wrong-signer revocation verified at the tool boundary rather than in prose.\n\nLISTANCHORS. Candidates come only from verified records with AnchorSchema; force, identity, scope and strength come from identity.Resolve at the frontier, never from the payload, and a candidate is dropped unless the resolved standing record is that exact anchor. Subject and scope filter by exact equality, deduplication is by record ID, the limit is bounded 1..100, and StandingAnchor carries no key material — record, subject, scope, resolved identity, strength strings and NotAfter. Head is a record ID. Truncation is set when a further matching standing record is found after the page fills, and chess_test.go:668 asserts exactly that with a bounded page.\n\nMY SECOND SUSPICION, ALSO WRONG. I expected truncation to be reported but never witnessed, since the tool-level test uses limit 1 against exactly one match. It is witnessed, at the library level, in the line above. Recorded because I would have flagged it as a gap had I stopped at the tool tests.\n\nSTRICT ARGUMENTS. Comprehensively covered and I checked the table rather than the claim: list_anchors refuses no filters, zero limit, limit 101, a 129-byte subject and an unknown field; revoke_anchor refuses a missing record, an empty record and an unknown field. tools/list coverage includes both new tools.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Host identity judgment stays outside chess.Decision — IdentityOutcome is a separate reader and says so in its own comment, rather than widening Decision to carry identity verdicts. The application and MCP contract does change, by adding two tools and changing what anchor returns, and README.md documents both in the same head. That is the right place for it. Chess still consumes gitseq/identity-anchor@0 rather than defining identity meaning of its own.\n\nSECURITY. The trust boundary is who may create or withdraw recovery authority, and the change strengthens it rather than widening it: authority answers come from the verified fold, signer standing is never inferred from a successful write, revocation force is measured against a counterfactual rather than assumed, read failures fail closed to unknown, filters are length- and content-bounded, the page is capped, and no custody or key material crosses the tool boundary. list_anchors is served from a read-only host.Open before the writer is opened, so listing needs no key. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Two observations, neither worth a round.\n\nFirst, the filter condition reads subject != \"\" \u0026\u0026 anchor.Subject != subject || scope != \"\" \u0026\u0026 anchor.Scope != scope. Go's precedence makes that correct — it is the disjunction of two mismatches — but it is unparenthesized and one reading away from inverting the filter. Parentheses would cost nothing.\n\nSecond, the revocation path rebuilds the set of anchored subjects by scanning every record in the log and JSON-unmarshalling each anchor payload, on every revocation outcome read. Identity mutations are rare and the log is small, so this is not a problem now. It is worth knowing that this read grows linearly with total history rather than with anchors currently standing, if the surface ever moves somewhere hot.\n\nGATES, run by me from a clean detached checkout at the exact head, worktree clean and byte-identical after both mutations were reverted: gofmt silent, git diff --check clean, go build ./... and go vet ./... exit 0, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with both packages ok — root 21.231s, cmd/chess 23.521s.\n\nCodex may merge this exact head once this verdict is ratified. The head is one commit ahead of main at 94f5c73 and zero behind, so there is no carry to audit. Any change to the head returns it to review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:887e9294761e19fc2936091d14e86723cd973e86","head":"c40dbf6958614a811ecd81790f695696ddf47205","verdict":"approved"}}